' MICROCHIP GOLF
' A round of golf for the ColecoVision, in Amy, by Amy Bienvenu, after
' CADDY in Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of Computer Games (Ballantine Books,
' 1984; Interface Publications, 1983): nine holes on "the Microchip
' Golfcourse", each with its DIFFICULTY FACTOR of three, four or five; the
' computer keeps the score card, tells you how each hole went and works
' out your average per hole; if you overshoot, your next shot goes back
' toward the hole.
'
' Here you stand behind the ball and look down the hole: the fairway with
' its mowing stripes, the rough and its trees on either side, bunkers, the
' green and the flag. Aim, swing, and the view flies with the ball.
'
' Controls (either joystick): left and right aim (the cross shows where);
' hold a side button to swing back - the power gauge rises and falls -
' and let go to hit. On the green you putt straight at the hole and only
' choose the strength. Either button goes through the pages; on the title
' and at the end of a round, up and down choose. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken (CADDY is a line of text with a "0" for the ball):
' - The holes keep the book's lengths (15 to 26 of its steps, shown here
'   as 225 to 390 yards) and its difficulty factor, which here makes the
'   wind (up to 6 mph a step of difficulty) and how short a shot may fall
'   (up to 4% a step; the book divided by up to 1 + the factor, which made
'   every stroke a lottery).
' - The book typed in a stroke strength; here it is the power gauge, and
'   the ball also goes left or right: aim, wind, rough (a shorter club),
'   bunkers (shorter still), and trees past the rough (a penalty stroke,
'   play again from where you were). On the green you putt; within a yard
'   of the hole the ball drops.
' - A hole is picked up after ten strokes. Par (3 up to 250 yards, else 4)
'   is new; so are the names for scores (birdie, bogey...).
' - New music (Morning on the Links) and sound effects.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22 (the course runs
' to the edges, as a picture); sprites: the club (two), the ball, the aim
' cross and four things on the course; the frame loop is protected from
' the NMI, and every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.
'
' The picture is Avalanche Run's slope engine (engine.asm): ground rows
' 8-23 coloured line by line from a depth table (the mowing stripes), the
' rough's edges drawn where they move, and the course's things projected
' as sprites in eight sizes. One depth step is 4 yards; sideways the units
' are feet.

